"""Money and tax pages. Facts are limited to pages opened on 27 October 2026."""

def add_pages(P, L, a, src, S):
    def note():
        return ('<div class="card warn"><p><strong>This is not tax advice.</strong> '
                'This page is general information for information purposes only. '
                'It is not legal advice and it is not tax advice. '
                'Confirm it with a qualified tax practitioner. '
                'If you have tax in both South Africa and the United States, speak to a cross-border tax practitioner. '
                'The sources on this page were read on 27 October 2026.</p></div>')

    f='tax-first-year/index.html'
    P(f,"Tax checklist when a refugee starts work in the USA","A step-by-step tax checklist for a South African employee who arrived as a refugee and is starting work in the United States.",f'''<h1>I was an employee in South Africa. I came as a refugee to find a job. What must I do about tax?</h1>
{note()}
<h2>Read this before the checklist</h2>
<div class="card"><p>This is the ordinary case. You worked for an employer in South Africa. You have come to the United States as a refugee to find a job. You are not using this page to decide what to do with a company, a rental property, or a director role.</p>
<p>Being admitted as a refugee is an immigration status. The Internal Revenue Service (IRS) says immigration status and tax residence are different questions. You are a United States resident for tax only if you meet the green card test or the substantial presence test{src("irs_p519","irs_visa")}.</p>
<p>If you also own companies or property in South Africa, or you are still a director, or you are deciding whether to move cash, read {L("sa-company-property/index.html","If you also own companies or property in South Africa",f)} after this checklist. The other money pages are {L("sa-tax-residency/index.html","SA tax residency",f)}, {L("us-tax-treaty/index.html","US tax and the treaty",f)}, {L("foreign-accounts/index.html","Reporting SA accounts",f)} and {L("moving-money/index.html","Moving money and pensions",f)}.</p></div>
<h2>South Africa: what to do</h2>
<div class="card"><ol>
<li><strong>Ask your old employer for your employees' tax certificate.</strong> The South African Revenue Service (SARS) says that where an employer pays remuneration, the employer must deduct employees' tax and, in most instances, give each employee an employees' tax certificate. That certificate is an IRP5 or an IT3(a). It shows, among other details, the employees' tax deducted. The employer guide says the certificate is due within the prescribed period. That paragraph does not print a number of days{src("sars_paye")}.</li>
<li><strong>Tell SARS if you have ceased to be a South African tax resident.</strong> SARS says a change in tax residence must be declared. For a person who was ordinarily resident, it is a factual question: whether your intention to stop making South Africa your real home is supported by objective factors. SARS lists those factors, including the visa you used, any proof of permanent residence, a certificate of tax residence from the other country if you have one, property and business interests you still have in South Africa, where your family is, and visits back. Flying to the United States does not, by itself, complete the declaration. You inform SARS on the Registration, Amendments and Verification Form (RAV01) on eFiling, by entering the date you ceased to be a tax resident. SARS then asks for a signed declaration and supporting documents. The steps are on {L("sa-tax-residency/index.html","SA tax residency",f)}{src("sars_cease")}.</li>
<li><strong>Know that the year you cease can include a deemed disposal.</strong> SARS says that when you break South African tax residence, you are deemed to dispose of your worldwide assets for capital gains tax, excluding immovable property situated in South Africa. After that, SARS taxes you on South African-source income, and no longer on worldwide income. SARS does not print a typical amount for an employee who held a bank account and a retirement fund. This page does not call that amount small or large. A practitioner calculates it. The plain explanation is on {L("sa-tax-residency/index.html","SA tax residency",f)}{src("sars_cease")}.</li>
<li><strong>Do not assume a South African return is either required or finished.</strong> The cease-residency page says the year you cease can include that deemed disposal, and that the change affects how later returns are assessed. It does not say, in one sentence, that every person who leaves must still file a return for the year of leaving, and it does not say the return falls away. Ask SARS or a practitioner whether a return is due for your year{src("sars_cease")}.</li>
<li><strong>Leave the retirement fund alone until you have read the fund rules.</strong> You do not cash a South African retirement fund in merely because you have boarded a flight. SARS says some withdrawals after you cease to be resident need an uninterrupted three years of non-residence, and a tax directive from SARS before the fund pays. Some parts of a fund follow a different rule. The breakdown is on {L("moving-money/index.html","Moving money and pensions",f)}{src("sars_directive","sars_ait")}.</li>
<li><strong>A South African bank account is a separate question from the tax return.</strong> The South African Reserve Bank (SARB) describes a non-resident rand account as the rand account of a non-resident. The pages checked do not say that ceasing tax residence forces you to close every South African account. Moving the money is a different set of rules, on {L("moving-money/index.html","Moving money and pensions",f)}{src("sarb_guide")}.</li>
</ol></div>
<h2>United States: what to do</h2>
<div class="card"><ol>
<li><strong>Work out whether you are a United States tax resident.</strong> The IRS uses two tests. The green card test is met if you are a lawful permanent resident at any time in the calendar year. The IRS says you generally have that status if USCIS has issued Form I-551, the green card. A refugee applies for a green card later, on Form I-485, after one year of physical presence. Until that card is issued, the green card test is not the test that makes you a resident{src("irs_p519","gc")}. The substantial presence test counts days you are physically in the United States: at least 31 days in the current year, and 183 days over three years, counting all of the days in the current year, one third of the days in the year before, and one sixth of the days in the year before that. Days in South Africa before you arrive are not United States days. In a first year, the count is the days you are here. The IRS list of exempt individuals, whose days are not counted, is limited to certain A, G, F, J, M and Q visa holders and certain professional athletes. Refugee is not on that list. The IRS lists refugees separately, and says their immigration status is not the same thing as tax residence{src("irs_spt","irs_p519","irs_visa")}.</li>
<li><strong>If you meet a test, the first year is often a dual-status year.</strong> Publication 519 says that if you are a United States resident for the calendar year, and you were not a resident at any time in the preceding year, you are a resident only from the residency starting date. You are a nonresident alien before that date. If you meet the substantial presence test, that starting date is generally the first day you are present in the United States during that year. You can exclude up to 10 days of presence when you set that date, if you show a closer connection to a foreign country on those days, and you file a statement. Those days still count toward the substantial presence test itself{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li><strong>If you do not meet the test in the arrival year, read the first-year choice before you assume you are a nonresident for the whole year.</strong> Publication 519 (2025) says a person who does not meet either test for 2024 or 2025, and who meets the substantial presence test for 2026, can choose to be treated as a United States resident for part of 2025. To make that choice the person must be present for at least 31 days in a row in 2025, and for at least 75 percent of the days from the first day of that period through the last day of 2025. Up to 5 days of absence can be treated as days of presence for the 75 percent test. The publication uses those years. Read the edition for the year you are filing. A practitioner applies the dates{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li><strong>There is a closer-connection exception, and it has a bar that matters to refugees.</strong> Even if you meet the substantial presence test, the IRS can treat you as a nonresident if you were present for fewer than 183 days during the year, you had a tax home in a foreign country for the year, and you had a closer connection to that country. You claim it on Form 8840. You cannot claim it if you applied to become a permanent resident, or if an application to adjust status was pending during the year. A refugee's green card application is Form I-485. Whether that application was pending in your year is a fact for a practitioner. If you were in the United States for 183 days or more, the first condition of this exception is not met{src("irs_p519","gc")}.</li>
<li><strong>Use a Social Security number if you can get one.</strong> The IRS says a person who is eligible for a Social Security number uses Form SS-5 to get it. An individual taxpayer identification number (ITIN), requested on Form W-7, is for a person who needs a taxpayer number and is not eligible for a Social Security number. Refugees can be issued a Social Security number. How the card arrives is on {L("social-security-card/index.html","Social Security card",f)}{src("irs_tin","irs_itin","ssnr")}.</li>
<li><strong>When the job starts, give the employer Form W-4.</strong> The IRS says you complete Form W-4, the Employee's Withholding Certificate, so the employer can withhold the correct federal income tax from your pay{src("irs_w4")}.</li>
<li><strong>File the federal return by the date IRS.gov gives for that year.</strong> If you are a resident alien, you report income on Form 1040. For a dual-status year, Publication 519 says you file Form 1040 or Form 1040-SR, check the "Other" box at the top, and write "Dual Status Return". You attach a statement of your United States-source income for the nonresident part of the year. You may use Form 1040-NR as that statement. The same chapter says you cannot file a joint return in a dual-status year, unless you make the choice to treat a nonresident spouse as a resident{src("irs_p519")}. Topic no. 301 says that if you are a calendar year filer and your tax year ends on 31 December, the due date for the federal individual income tax return is generally 15 April of each year. If the due date falls on a Saturday, Sunday, or legal holiday, the due date moves to the next business day. The same page says the due date for the 2025 tax return is 15 April 2026. That day has passed. Topic no. 301 does not print a due date for the 2026 tax year return. The individual filing page, last reviewed on 27 June 2026, still says 15 April 2026 is the deadline to file and pay, and it does not name the tax year. Open IRS.gov again for the year you file{src("irs_tc301","irs_file")}.</li>
<li><strong>A resident alien is taxed on worldwide income.</strong> Publication 519 says resident aliens are generally taxed on worldwide income, in the same way as United States citizens. That includes pay from the United States job and income that still comes from South Africa, such as interest. A nonresident alien is taxed on income from sources in the United States and on certain income connected with a United States trade or business. United States wages are in that United States-source group. Do not read "not yet a resident" as "no United States tax on the job"{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li><strong>If South Africa taxed the same income, look at the foreign tax credit.</strong> The IRS says that if you paid or accrued foreign income tax and you are subject to United States tax on the same income, you may be able to take a credit or an itemized deduction. Individuals claim the credit on Form 1116. If you exclude the income, you cannot also take the credit for the tax on the excluded part{src("irs_ftc")}. Form 2555, the foreign earned income exclusion, is for a person who has a tax home in a foreign country and who meets either the bona fide residence test or the physical presence test (330 full days in a foreign country during 12 months in a row). It is the form for foreign earned income, not for wages from a job in the United States. Whether any South African wages from before you moved can be excluded is a question for a practitioner. The treaty rules are on {L("us-tax-treaty/index.html","US tax and the treaty",f)}{src("irs_2555")}.</li>
<li><strong>Check the foreign-account thresholds, even for one savings account.</strong> FinCEN Form 114, the Report of Foreign Bank and Financial Accounts (FBAR), is required if you are a United States person and the total of your foreign financial accounts went over $10,000 at any time in the calendar year. Form 8938 is a different form, attached to the tax return, with higher thresholds. Both are explained on {L("foreign-accounts/index.html","Reporting SA accounts",f)}{src("irs_fbar","fincen_fbar","irs_8938")}.</li>
<li><strong>Free help exists, and a site may not cover a dual-status return.</strong> Volunteer Income Tax Assistance (VITA) offers free basic preparation. The IRS does not set one income limit for every site. Many sites help people who generally make about $70,244 or less, people with disabilities, and people with limited English. Ask the site whether it can prepare a dual-status return or a foreign-account form{src("irs_vita")}. IRS Free File guided software, in the IRS news release of 20 March 2026, is free for a taxpayer whose adjusted gross income is $89,000 or less. You must start from the IRS Free File page. Free File Fillable Forms are available regardless of income for people who are comfortable preparing their own return. That $89,000 figure is the one the IRS published for that filing season. Check the Free File page for the year you file{src("irs_freefile")}.</li>
</ol></div>
<h2>If you live in Florida</h2>
<div class="card"><p>This note is only for Florida. The Florida Department of Revenue says Florida does not impose a personal income tax, so there is no Florida personal income tax return to file. The same department's brochure for new residents says Florida does not impose personal income tax. You still have the federal return. This guide does not state the income tax rule of any other state{src("fl_pit","fl_new")}.</p></div>
<h2>If you also own companies or property in South Africa</h2>
<div class="card"><p>Stop using this checklist as your only page if any of these are true: you own shares in a South African company, the company holds property, you are still a director, or you are deciding whether to leave cash in South Africa or bring it to the United States. Those questions, and the forms the IRS and SARS name, are on {L("sa-company-property/index.html","A company or property",f)}. They are things to consider. They are not advice to sell, to resign, or to move the money.</p></div>
<h2>What this page leaves out</h2>
<div class="card"><ul>
<li>A dollar amount for the United States filing threshold. The Form 1040 instructions change, and this page does not copy a gross-income figure from a year it did not open for that purpose.</li>
<li>A number of days for the IRP5. The employer guide says "the prescribed period" in the paragraph checked{src("sars_paye")}.</li>
<li>A statement that the deemed disposal is usually small. SARS does not say that on the cease-residency page{src("sars_cease")}.</li>
<li>State income tax outside Florida.</li>
</ul></div>''')

    f='sa-tax-residency/index.html'
    P(f,"Ceasing South African tax residency after you leave","How SARS decides that you have ceased to be a South African tax resident, how you declare it, and what the deemed disposal is.",f'''<h1>Ceasing to be a South African tax resident</h1>
{note()}
<p>Start with {L("tax-first-year/index.html","Tax when you start work",f)} if you were an ordinary employee. This page is the SARS detail behind steps 2, 3 and 4 of that checklist.</p>
<h2>Who is a South African tax resident</h2>
<div class="card"><p>From 1 March 2001, South Africa has taxed residents on worldwide income, with exemptions the Act allows, and has taxed non-residents on income from a South African source{src("sars_cease")}.</p>
<ul>
<li>You can be a resident because you are ordinarily resident. SARS says that idea is not clearly defined. It is decided case by case. Interpretation Note 3 lists the factors{src("sars_cease")}.</li>
<li>You can be a resident because of the physical presence test, even if you are not ordinarily resident. The count is in Interpretation Note 4. A SARS brochure for non-residents states the three parts: more than 91 days in the year of assessment, more than 91 days in each of the five preceding years of assessment, and more than 915 days in total during those five years. A year of assessment runs from 1 March to the last day of February. A day includes part of a day. The United States tax year is a calendar year. The two years do not start on the same day{src("sars_cease","sars_brochure")}.</li>
<li>A person who is treated as exclusively a resident of the other country under a double tax agreement is excluded from the South African definition of resident, even if the ordinary-residence or physical-presence test would otherwise be met{src("sars_cease")}.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>How you tell SARS you have ceased</h2>
<div class="card"><p>For a person who was ordinarily resident, SARS says the question is whether your subjective intention to cease to be ordinarily resident, and to stop making South Africa your real home, is supported by objective factors. The date is the day you ceased that residence. The factors SARS lists are the visa, proof of permanent residence if you have it, a certificate or letter of tax residence from the foreign authority if one is available, property you still have in South Africa and what it is used for, business interests, where your family is, social interests and personal belongings, and the frequency of and reasons for return visits{src("sars_cease")}.</p>
<p>A person who is resident only under the physical presence test ceases to be resident after a continuous period of at least 330 full days outside South Africa. SARS deems that person to have ceased to be resident from the day they left{src("sars_cease")}.</p>
<p>A person who becomes a tax resident of another country under a double tax agreement also ceases to be a South African tax resident{src("sars_cease")}.</p>
<p>You declare the date on form RAV01 on eFiling, under Income Tax Liability Details. SARS opens a case and asks for documents. The standard documents are a signed declaration of the basis you rely on, a letter of motivation, and a copy of your passport or travel diary, including the pages with entry and exit stamps. Extra documents depend on whether you ceased to be ordinarily resident, ceased under the physical presence test, or ceased because of a double tax agreement. If you are not on eFiling, SARS says you may use the contact email printed on that page. SARS can decline the declaration if you do not meet the criteria or you do not provide the documents it asks for{src("sars_cease")}.</p></div>
<h2>The deemed disposal, in plain words</h2>
<div class="card"><p>SARS says a deemed disposal for capital gains tax takes place when you break tax residence. You are deemed to have disposed of your worldwide assets, excluding immovable property situated in South Africa. The international-transfer page asks for a capital gains calculation of the tax on that deemed disposal on the day before you ceased to be resident, and it cites section 9(H)(2) of the Income Tax Act. A SARS interpretation note discusses the same rule as section 9H and says the exclusion of immovable property does not extend to shares that paragraph 2(2) treats as an interest in immovable property, including equity shares in a land-rich company{src("sars_cease","sars_ait","sars_in43")}.</p>
<p>The cease-residency page does not say the deemed disposal is usually small, and it does not say whether an interest in a retirement fund is inside that disposal. Those points are left out of this guide. After you have ceased, SARS says you are taxed only on South African-source income{src("sars_cease")}.</p></div>
<h2>The year you leave</h2>
<div class="card"><p>SARS says the declaration changes the basis on which you are taxed and how returns are assessed after that. The year you cease may include the deemed capital gains disposal, depending on the assets and where they were. The employer still has the IRP5 duty described on the {L("tax-first-year/index.html","checklist",f)}{src("sars_cease","sars_paye")}.</p>
<p>Whether you must file a South African return for the year of leaving: not stated as a yes or no on the cease-residency page. Ask SARS or a practitioner{src("sars_cease")}.</p></div>
<h2>The 2021 change from financial emigration</h2>
<div class="card"><p>On 1 March 2021 SARS said the exchange-control idea of "emigration", and the South African Reserve Bank approval on form MP336(b), would end. From that date, the tax event that matters is ceasing to be a resident for tax purposes. Applications whose MP336(b) was attested by an authorised dealer on or before 28 February 2021 could still use the old emigration tax-compliance process until 28 February 2022. From 1 March 2021, other cases are processed by SARS confirming that the person has ceased to be a tax resident. An MP336(b) is no longer part of that application. SARB's guidelines for individuals say the same phase-out date: the concept of emigration recognised by the Financial Surveillance Department was phased out with effect from 1 March 2021{src("sars_tcs2021","sarb_guide")}.</p>
<p>Retirement-fund access changed with that shift. The detail, including the three-year rule and the parts of a fund that do not use it, is on {L("moving-money/index.html","Moving money and pensions",f)}.</p></div>
<h2>Conflicts and gaps</h2>
<div class="card conf"><ul>
<li>The cease-residency page describes the capital-gains exclusion as immovable property in South Africa. The interpretation note says that exclusion does not cover land-rich company shares that count as an interest in immovable property. Both documents are SARS documents. Read both{src("sars_cease","sars_in43")}.</li>
<li>The international-transfer page writes the section as 9(H)(2). The interpretation note writes section 9H. This guide treats those as the same provision written two ways{src("sars_ait","sars_in43")}.</li>
<li>What a retirement-fund interest does inside the deemed disposal: not stated on the cease-residency page.</li>
</ul></div>''')

    f='us-tax-treaty/index.html'
    P(f,"United States tax residency and the South Africa treaty","When the IRS treats a new arrival as a resident alien, and how the South Africa income tax treaty deals with residence, wages, pensions and double tax.",f'''<h1>United States tax residence and the treaty with South Africa</h1>
{note()}
<p>The short version for a person who was an employee is on {L("tax-first-year/index.html","Tax when you start work",f)}. This page is the IRS and treaty detail.</p>
<h2>Green card test and substantial presence test</h2>
<div class="card"><p>Publication 519 says an alien (a person who is not a United States citizen) is a nonresident alien unless they meet the green card test or the substantial presence test. You can be both a nonresident and a resident in the same year. That is dual status{src("irs_p519")}.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Green card test.</strong> You are a resident if you are a lawful permanent resident at any time during the calendar year. You generally have that status if USCIS has issued Form I-551. You keep it unless the status is taken away or is determined to have been abandoned{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li><strong>Substantial presence test.</strong> At least 31 days in the current year, and 183 days over the current year and the two years before it. Count every day in the current year, one third of the days in the first year before it, and one sixth of the days in the year before that. Any part of a day in the United States counts, with the exceptions the IRS lists. Exempt individuals do not count days, and the IRS list is certain foreign-government people on A or G visas (not A-3 or G-5), teachers and trainees on J or Q visas, students on F, J, M or Q visas, and professional athletes in a charitable sports event. Refugee is not on that list{src("irs_spt","irs_p519")}.</li>
<li>The IRS page on taxation by visa type says a nonimmigrant or immigrant status under immigration law is different from tax residence. It lists refugees as one category of alien. It does not create a separate refugee tax status{src("irs_visa")}.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>The first year and dual status</h2>
<div class="card"><p>If you are a United States resident for the year, and you were not a resident at any time in the year before, Publication 519 says you are a resident only from the residency starting date. Before that date you are a nonresident alien{src("irs_p519")}.</p>
<ul>
<li>If you meet the substantial presence test, the starting date is generally the first day you are present during that year. You may exclude up to 10 days if your tax home was in a foreign country and you had a closer connection to that country on those days. You must file a signed statement. The excluded days still count for the substantial presence test{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li>If you meet the green card test and not the substantial presence test, the starting date is the first day you are present as a lawful permanent resident. If you meet both, it is the earlier of the two dates{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li>For the dual-status return, file Form 1040 or Form 1040-SR, mark "Other", and write "Dual Status Return". Attach a statement of United States-source income for the nonresident part. Form 1040-NR may be that statement. You cannot file a joint return unless you make the choice, described in the same chapter, to treat a nonresident spouse as a resident. That choice taxes both of you on worldwide income for the whole year{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li>The closer-connection exception and the first-year choice are summarised on the {L("tax-first-year/index.html","checklist",f)}. The conditions, including the bar if an adjustment-of-status application was pending, are in Publication 519{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
</ul>
<p>Resident aliens are taxed on worldwide income. Nonresident aliens are taxed on United States-source income and on certain income connected with a United States trade or business{src("irs_p519")}.</p></div>
<h2>Worldwide income, the credit, and the exclusion</h2>
<div class="card"><ul>
<li><strong>Foreign tax credit.</strong> If foreign income tax was paid or accrued and the same income is subject to United States tax, you may take a credit or an itemized deduction. The credit reduces United States tax. Individuals use Form 1116. You cannot take a credit for tax on income you exclude under the foreign earned income exclusion. If a treaty gives you a lower foreign tax rate, only that lower tax qualifies for the credit{src("irs_ftc")}.</li>
<li><strong>Foreign earned income exclusion.</strong> Form 2555 is available if you have a tax home in a foreign country and you meet the bona fide residence test or the physical presence test. The physical presence test is 330 full days in a foreign country during any 12 months in a row. The bona fide residence test, for a resident alien, requires that you are a citizen or national of a country that has an income tax treaty with the United States, and that you are a bona fide resident of a foreign country for an uninterrupted period that includes a whole tax year. Wages you earn from a job in the United States are not foreign earned income under that form. The instructions say you cannot exclude more than your foreign earned income for the year{src("irs_2555")}.</li>
<li><strong>Social Security number and ITIN.</strong> If you are eligible for a Social Security number, that is the taxpayer number you use. Form W-7 is for a person who is not eligible for one{src("irs_tin","irs_itin")}.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>The treaty tie-breaker</h2>
<div class="card"><p>The income tax convention between the United States and South Africa is published by the IRS. It was generally effective from 1 January 1998. Article 4 defines a resident. For South Africa, that is an individual who is ordinarily resident in South Africa, and a company that is incorporated or has its place of effective management in South Africa. For the United States, it is a person liable to tax there by reason of domicile, residence, citizenship, place of incorporation, or a similar criterion, with the limits written in the article{src("irs_treaty","irs_treatypage")}.</p>
<p>If the tests of both countries make an individual a resident of both, Article 4 sets this order{src("irs_treaty")}:</p>
<ol>
<li>The country where the person has a permanent home. If there is a permanent home in both, the country where the personal and economic relations are closer. The treaty calls that the centre of vital interests.</li>
<li>If that centre cannot be determined, or there is a permanent home in neither country, the country where the person has a habitual abode.</li>
<li>If there is a habitual abode in both or in neither, the country of nationality.</li>
<li>If the person is a national of both or of neither, the competent authorities settle it.</li>
</ol>
<p>Publication 519 says the green card test and the substantial presence test do not override a treaty. If a treaty's tie-breaker treats you as a resident of the other country, you are treated as a nonresident alien when the United States figures your tax. For other purposes you are still treated as a United States resident. The publication gives an example: residency time periods for dual-status rules are not changed by that treaty treatment{src("irs_p519")}.</p>
<p>Article 23 says the United States allows a resident or citizen a credit against United States tax for South African tax paid or accrued, subject to the limitations of United States law. That is the treaty side of the Form 1116 credit{src("irs_treaty","irs_ftc")}.</p></div>
<h2>Pensions and wages under the treaty</h2>
<div class="card"><p><strong>Wages.</strong> Article 15 says salaries and wages that a resident of one country earns from employment are taxable only in that country, unless the work is exercised in the other country. If the work is exercised in the other country, that country may tax the pay. A further paragraph can return the taxing right to the country of residence if three conditions are all met, including a stay of not more than 183 days. Read the article before you rely on it. It is not a reason to skip the United States return on wages from a job in the United States{src("irs_treaty")}.</p>
<p><strong>Pensions.</strong> Article 18 deals with pensions and annuities. The country that is the source may tax a pension paid to a resident of the other country, on the conditions written in the article. Where the United States is the source, the treaty caps the tax at 15 percent of the gross amount if the pension is not subject to an early-withdrawal penalty. Where South Africa is the source, the treaty allows South African tax if the person was employed in South Africa for periods totalling at least two years in the ten years before the pension first became due, and for periods totalling at least ten years. Social security benefits paid by one country to a resident of the other are taxable only in the paying country. The full pension rules for a South African fund are on {L("moving-money/index.html","Moving money and pensions",f)}{src("irs_treaty")}.</p></div>
<h2>Conflicts and gaps</h2>
<div class="card conf"><ul>
<li>Publication 519 (2025) writes the first-year choice with the years 2024, 2025 and 2026. This guide does not rewrite that choice for a later year. Use the edition for the return you are filing{src("irs_p519")}.</li>
<li>The treaty and the Internal Revenue Code can point different ways. Publication 519 says a treaty tie-breaker can treat you as a nonresident for the tax computation and as a resident for other purposes. Do not collapse those into one label{src("irs_p519","irs_treaty")}.</li>
</ul></div>''')

    f='foreign-accounts/index.html'
    P(f,"Reporting South African bank accounts on FBAR and 8938","When FinCEN Form 114 and IRS Form 8938 apply to South African accounts, what the thresholds are, and what penalties the agencies publish.",f'''<h1>Reporting South African accounts: FBAR and Form 8938</h1>
{note()}
<p>These forms are reports. They are not, by themselves, a tax on the account. Interest on the account is a separate question, covered on {L("us-tax-treaty/index.html","US tax and the treaty",f)} and {L("tax-first-year/index.html","the checklist",f)}.</p>
<h2>FinCEN Form 114, the FBAR</h2>
<div class="card"><p>The Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) says a United States person who has a financial interest in, or signature authority over, foreign financial accounts must file a Report of Foreign Bank and Financial Accounts if the aggregate value of those accounts exceeds $10,000 at any time during the calendar year. An individual can file electronically through the BSA E-Filing System{src("fincen_fbar")}.</p>
<p>The IRS page says the same $10,000 aggregate test, and it says a United States person includes a citizen and a resident. The report is due on 15 April following the calendar year. If you miss 15 April, you have an automatic extension to 15 October. You do not file a form to get that extension. The FBAR is not filed with the income tax return{src("irs_fbar")}.</p>
<p>The IRS describes a foreign financial account as an account at a financial institution outside the United States. Reportable accounts include bank accounts. The pages checked do not print a special rule that leaves out a South African bank account{src("irs_fbar","fincen_fbar")}.</p></div>
<h2>Form 8938</h2>
<div class="card"><p>Form 8938, Statement of Specified Foreign Financial Assets, is attached to the income tax return. The IRS says you do not file it if you do not have to file an income tax return for the year, even if the assets are above the threshold{src("irs_8938")}.</p>
<p>Specified foreign financial assets include a financial account at a foreign financial institution, and other foreign financial assets held for investment that are not in an account. Those other assets include stock or securities issued by someone other than a United States person, an interest in a foreign entity, and a financial instrument or contract with an issuer or counterparty that is not a United States person. Shares in a South African company can fall in that list. The "do I need to file" page does not list a house you hold in your own name as one of those assets{src("irs_8938")}.</p>
<p>Thresholds for a person living in the United States{src("irs_8938")}:</p>
<ul>
<li>Unmarried, or married filing a separate return: more than $50,000 on the last day of the tax year, or more than $75,000 at any time during the year.</li>
<li>Married filing a joint return: more than $100,000 on the last day, or more than $150,000 at any time during the year.</li>
</ul>
<p>The thresholds are higher if you are a taxpayer living abroad, as the IRS defines that phrase (a tax home in a foreign country and either bona fide residence for the whole year, or 330 days of presence in a foreign country in 12 months). Unmarried or not filing jointly: more than $200,000 on the last day, or more than $300,000 at any time. Joint return: more than $400,000 on the last day, or more than $600,000 at any time{src("irs_8938")}.</p>
<p>A person who has moved to the United States and lives here uses the "living in the United States" thresholds, unless they meet the IRS definition of living abroad. Do not use the higher figures only because the account is in South Africa{src("irs_8938")}.</p>
<p>The instructions say certain tax-favoured foreign trusts, set up to provide pension or retirement benefits, can be exempt from Form 3520 and Form 3520-A under Revenue Procedure 2020-17. That exemption does not remove a Form 8938 duty{src("irs_i8938")}.</p></div>
<h2>The two forms are not the same report</h2>
<div class="card"><p>The FBAR goes to FinCEN on FinCEN Form 114. Form 8938 goes to the IRS with the income tax return. The dollar tests are different. An account can be over the FBAR line and under the Form 8938 line, or over both. Filing one form is not described, on either page, as a substitute for the other{src("irs_fbar","irs_8938","fincen_fbar")}.</p></div>
<h2>Penalties the agencies publish</h2>
<div class="card"><p><strong>FBAR.</strong> The IRS says you may face civil and criminal penalties for FBAR reporting or recordkeeping failures, and that the civil maximums in Title 31 of the United States Code are adjusted each year for inflation. An IRS practice paper states the structure this way: a non-willful failure can be penalised by up to $10,000, adjusted for inflation, unless there is reasonable cause and the account was properly reported on a late FBAR. The same paper says the Supreme Court, in Bittner v. United States, held that a non-willful failure to file the report is one violation, not a separate penalty for each account. A willful failure can be penalised by the greater of $100,000, adjusted for inflation, or 50 percent of the balance in the account at the time of the violation. This guide does not copy a current inflation-adjusted dollar amount. The IRS points to the adjustment table in Title 31 of the Code of Federal Regulations for that figure{src("irs_fbar","irs_fbarpen")}.</p>
<p><strong>Form 8938.</strong> The IRS says the penalty for not filing a complete and correct Form 8938 by the due date is $10,000. If the IRS mails a notice and you do not file within 90 days, a further $10,000 can apply for each 30-day period after those 90 days. The maximum of that continuation penalty is $50,000. No penalty is imposed if the failure is due to reasonable cause and not to willful neglect. You have to show the facts{src("irs_intpen")}.</p></div>
<h2>Conflicts and gaps</h2>
<div class="card conf"><ul>
<li>The statute prints $10,000 and $100,000. The IRS says those maximums are adjusted for inflation. This page does not invent the adjusted number{src("irs_fbar","irs_fbarpen")}.</li>
<li>Whether the year you arrive is an FBAR year if you are a resident for only part of it: the IRS FBAR page says a United States person includes a resident, and it does not, on that page, spell out a dual-status year. Ask a practitioner{src("irs_fbar")}.</li>
<li>A house in your own name is not in the Form 8938 asset list quoted above. Shares in a company that owns the house can be. The company page is {L("sa-company-property/index.html","A company or property",f)}{src("irs_8938")}.</li>
</ul></div>''')

    f='moving-money/index.html'
    P(f,"Moving South African savings and retirement fund money","SARB allowances, the SARS approval for an international transfer, and what SARS says about retirement funds after you cease to be a tax resident.",f'''<h1>Moving money and retirement savings</h1>
{note()}
<p>This page says what the official rules are. It does not say whether you should move money. If the question is "leave the cash in South Africa or bring it to the United States", the things to weigh are on {L("sa-company-property/index.html","A company or property",f)}.</p>
<h2>Allowances while you are still a South African resident</h2>
<div class="card"><p>Two SARB documents, and the SARB questions-and-answers page, do not print the same rand amount for the single discretionary allowance. The conflict is in the box at the end of this page. Read that box before you use either number at a bank{src("sarb_circ","sarb_guide","sarb_faq")}.</p>
<p>What the documents agree on is the shape of the rules for a resident{src("sarb_guide","sarb_faq","sarb_circ")}:</p>
<ul>
<li>A single discretionary allowance is available to a resident natural person of 18 or older, for any legal purpose abroad, including investment. The authorised dealer is a bank that may deal in foreign exchange.</li>
<li>A separate foreign capital allowance of up to R10 million per calendar year can be transferred for investment, if you are 18 or older and tax compliant. The bank checks a SARS tax compliance status PIN before it sends the money. SARB's 2025 circular says that PIN is tied to an Approval for International Transfer (AIT), which is the SARS name{src("sarb_circ12","sars_ait")}.</li>
<li>More than R10 million in a calendar year needs an application through the bank to the Financial Surveillance Department, with the SARS tax compliance result{src("sarb_faq")}.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>Transfers after you cease to be a tax resident</h2>
<div class="card"><p>SARB says that from 1 March 2021 it no longer treats "emigration" as an exchange-control concept. The distinction between resident assets and non-resident assets remains. An authorised dealer may allow a transfer abroad if you have ceased to be a South African tax resident, you have the tax compliance status SARS requires, and the bank verifies that you are tax compliant{src("sarb_guide","sars_tcs2021")}.</p>
<p>The individuals guide then sets these limits. Where a later circular changes a rand amount, the conflict box says so{src("sarb_guide","sarb_circ")}:</p>
<ul>
<li>In the calendar year you cease to be resident, a once-off travel allowance can be transferred without a tax compliance PIN. It cannot be used again in a later year. The guide says a person who ceases to be resident qualifies for that travel allowance and may not use any unused part of the single discretionary allowance that residents have{src("sarb_guide")}.</li>
<li>The guide allows up to R10 million per calendar year, with the tax compliance application, for a person of 18 or older who has ceased to be a tax resident. Above R10 million, SARS does a stricter check and the Financial Surveillance Department also approves{src("sarb_guide")}.</li>
<li>On a once-off basis, remaining cash of not more than R100 000 in total may be sent abroad without reference to SARS{src("sarb_guide")}.</li>
<li>Income that is legitimately due, including interest, dividends, directors' fees, rentals and pensions from registered funds, can be transferred if the bank confirms it is due and checks tax compliance. The guide lists the income types and the checks{src("sarb_guide")}.</li>
</ul>
<p>SARS, for an Approval for International Transfer by a person who has ceased to be resident, asks for proof of the cessation and the date, and for a capital gains calculation of the tax on the deemed disposal under the section it writes as 9(H)(2){src("sars_ait")}.</p></div>
<h2>Retirement funds</h2>
<div class="card"><p>Before 1 March 2021, access to some retirement money after leaving was tied to emigration that SARB recognised. SARS says that exchange-control emigration approval ended for new cases on 1 March 2021. From then, the test SARS describes is an uninterrupted period as a non-resident for tax purposes, plus a tax directive. The fund, the administrator or the insurer must get the directive before it pays a lump sum. A directive application on or after 1 September 2024 that uses the old reason "Emigration Withdrawal" is no longer accepted{src("sars_tcs2021","sars_directive")}.</p>
<p>The directive guide and the international-transfer page describe the three-year rule by type of fund and by component. They were both read. Where they use different words, both are quoted{src("sars_directive","sars_ait")}.</p>
<ul>
<li>A retirement annuity: from 1 March 2021, a member who has ceased to be resident for an uninterrupted three years or longer, and who has stopped contributing, can withdraw before retirement. From 1 September 2024 the guide says the full value in the vested component and the retirement component may be accessed for this reason. The international-transfer page says access to the vested component of a retirement annuity is subject to three years of non-residence, and access to the retirement component of any retirement fund is subject to three years{src("sars_directive","sars_ait")}.</li>
<li>A pension fund or provident fund: from 1 September 2024, a member who has been non-resident for an uninterrupted three years or longer may withdraw the full value in the retirement component before retirement. The international-transfer page says access to the vested component of a pension fund or a provident fund is not subject to that three-year period{src("sars_directive","sars_ait")}.</li>
<li>The savings component: the international-transfer page says access to the savings component of any retirement fund does not require three years of non-residence{src("sars_ait")}.</li>
<li>A preservation fund: the guide's three-year rules differ according to whether you have already taken the one-time withdrawal. Read the guide for the fund you actually have. This page does not collapse those cases into one sentence{src("sars_directive")}.</li>
</ul>
<p>The fund will ask for proof. The directive guide says the application includes a certificate of residence, not older than 12 months, from the tax authority of the country where you are resident{src("sars_directive")}.</p>
<p>SARB says an authorised dealer may allow the withdrawal of lump-sum benefits from a pension preservation fund, a provident preservation fund and a retirement annuity fund only if the member has remained a non-tax resident for at least three consecutive years, and the transfer rules above R10 million still apply where the amount requires them{src("sarb_guide")}.</p></div>
<h2>Pensions under the treaty</h2>
<div class="card"><p>Article 18 of the United States and South Africa income tax convention says the source country may tax a pension paid to a resident of the other country, on the conditions in the article. For a pension from South Africa, those conditions include employment in South Africa for at least two years in total in the ten years before the pension first became due, and at least ten years in total. Social security and similar public pensions are taxable only in the country that pays them. The article is quoted more fully on {L("us-tax-treaty/index.html","US tax and the treaty",f)}{src("irs_treaty")}.</p>
<p>If you are a United States tax resident, a pension that South Africa also taxes can be looked at for the foreign tax credit on Form 1116. That is a credit question, not a reason to skip either return{src("irs_ftc","irs_treaty")}.</p></div>
<h2>A South African bank account</h2>
<div class="card"><p>SARB's guidelines define a non-resident rand account as the rand account of a non-resident, kept with an authorised dealer. They do not say, in the sections checked, that a person who has ceased to be a tax resident must close every South African bank account. Whether a particular bank will keep the account is not stated on the SARB pages{src("sarb_guide")}.</p>
<p>If you are a United States tax resident, interest on that account is part of worldwide income. If the accounts cross the FBAR or Form 8938 lines, the reports on {L("foreign-accounts/index.html","Reporting SA accounts",f)} can apply as well{src("irs_p519","irs_fbar","irs_8938")}.</p></div>
<h2>Conflicts and gaps</h2>
<div class="card conf"><ul>
<li><strong>Single discretionary allowance: R1 million or R2 million.</strong> Exchange Control Circular No. 6/2026, dated 8 April 2026, says the single discretionary allowance for a resident of 18 or older was R1 million and is increased to R2 million from the date of the circular. It also raises the once-off travel allowance in the year you cease to be a tax resident from the old figure to R2 million, and the household-and-personal-effects figure in that year to R2 million. The Currency and Exchanges Guidelines for Individuals, in the 2026 folder on the same website, still print R1 million for the single discretionary allowance and R1 million for that once-off travel allowance. The Financial Surveillance questions-and-answers page also still says R1 million. The foreign capital allowance of R10 million is in the circular and in the guidelines. The bank and a practitioner should be shown all three pages. This guide does not pick a winner{src("sarb_circ","sarb_guide","sarb_faq")}.</li>
<li>SARB's individuals guide still speaks of a tax compliance status in respect of "emigration". SARS, from 1 March 2021, says the MP336(b) emigration approval is not the test, and its transfer page now uses Approval for International Transfer. SARB Circular 12/2025 adds "AIT" as the name for that SARS approval. The words on the public guides have not all been updated to one label{src("sarb_guide","sars_tcs2021","sarb_circ12","sars_ait")}.</li>
<li>The directive guide and the international-transfer page split pension, provident, preservation and retirement-annuity money into components, and they do not use one sentence for every component. Quote the page that matches the fund{src("sars_directive","sars_ait")}.</li>
</ul></div>''')

    f='sa-company-property/index.html'
    P(f,"SA company, property or director role after you move","What to consider, and which official forms apply, if you still own a South African company or property, remain a director, or are deciding where to keep cash.",f'''<h1>If you still have a company, property, or cash in South Africa</h1>
{note()}
<h2>What to consider, not what to do</h2>
<div class="card"><p>The ordinary case, an employee who has come to find a job, is {L("tax-first-year/index.html","Tax when you start work",f)}. This page is for three further questions people ask:</p>
<ul>
<li>I own properties through companies in South Africa.</li>
<li>I am still a director of a South African company.</li>
<li>Should I move my cash to the United States, or leave it in South Africa?</li>
</ul>
<p>The pages below name the official forms and the official rules. They do not tell you to sell, to resign, to keep the shares, or to move the money. A cross-border tax practitioner applies them to your facts.</p></div>
<h2>I own properties through companies</h2>
<div class="card"><p><strong>United States information returns.</strong> If you are a United States person and you own shares in a foreign corporation, the IRS instructions for Form 5471, Information Return of U.S. Persons With Respect to Certain Foreign Corporations (revision of December 2025), set out categories of filers. There are exceptions in the instructions. A category is not a decision that you must file{src("irs_5471")}:</p>
<ul>
<li>Category 1 is a United States shareholder of a foreign corporation that was a section 965 specified foreign corporation, on the terms in the instructions.</li>
<li>Category 2 includes a United States citizen or resident who is an officer or director of a foreign corporation in which a United States person has acquired stock that meets the 10 percent stock ownership requirement, or an additional 10 percent or more.</li>
<li>Category 3 includes a United States person who acquires stock to meet the 10 percent requirement, a person who becomes a United States person while meeting that requirement, and a United States person who disposes of enough stock to fall below it. Becoming a United States tax resident while you already own 10 percent is one of the cases the instructions list.</li>
<li>Category 4 is a United States person who had control of a foreign corporation. Control, for this category, is more than 50 percent of the voting power or more than 50 percent of the value.</li>
<li>Category 5 is a United States shareholder who owned stock in a controlled foreign corporation on the last day in the year on which it was a controlled foreign corporation. For this category, a United States shareholder owns 10 percent or more of the voting power or value. A controlled foreign corporation is a foreign corporation in which United States shareholders own, on any day of its tax year, more than 50 percent of the voting power or more than 50 percent of the value.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same instructions say United States shareholders of a controlled foreign corporation that has subpart F income must report that income on their tax returns. Schedule I-1 of Form 5471 is information used for the inclusion under section 951A. The instructions tell the shareholder to use Form 8992 for that calculation, and they call it global intangible low-taxed income (GILTI). The IRS draft forms page lists draft Instructions for Form 8992 with a posted date of 21 October 2026. The file is marked draft and not for filing. Its heading uses the revision label December 2026, and a line in the file prints 19 October 2026. That draft says Public Law 119-21 renamed GILTI to net CFC tested income (NCTI) for taxable years beginning after 31 December 2025, and changed the calculation. The About Form 8992 page, reviewed on 22 July 2026, still describes the form as the GILTI calculation. December 2026 is the revision label printed on the draft. It is not a month this guide treats as already reached. Check which text applies to the year you file. This guide does not restate the formula{src("irs_5471","irs_8992","irs_8992dft","irs_drafts")}.</p>
<p><strong>A passive foreign investment company.</strong> Form 8621 is the information return for a shareholder of a passive foreign investment company. The instructions say a foreign corporation is one if 75 percent or more of its gross income for the year is passive income, or if at least 50 percent of its assets produce passive income or are held to produce it. A United States person files Form 8621 in the five situations the instructions list, including certain distributions, certain dispositions, and an annual report under section 1298(f). There is also an overlap rule for stock that is both a controlled foreign corporation and a passive foreign investment company. This page does not decide that a company which holds South African property meets either test{src("irs_8621")}.</p>
<p><strong>Form 8938.</strong> An interest in a foreign entity, and stock issued by someone other than a United States person, can be specified foreign financial assets. The thresholds are on {L("foreign-accounts/index.html","Reporting SA accounts",f)}{src("irs_8938")}.</p>
<p><strong>South African tax on the shares and the property.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dividends.</strong> SARS says dividends tax is 20 percent, from 22 February 2017, on dividends paid by a resident company, and that the rate can be reduced by a double tax agreement. The non-resident beneficial owner submits the declaration forms on the SARS website to the company or the regulated intermediary, or the tax is withheld. The treaty, Article 10, caps the tax at 5 percent of the gross dividend if the beneficial owner is a company that holds directly at least 10 percent of the voting stock, and at 15 percent in all other cases. An individual shareholder is in the 15 percent group. The treaty rate is a cap. SARS still requires the declaration{src("sars_nr","irs_treaty")}.</li>
<li><strong>Capital gains for a non-resident.</strong> SARS says a non-resident is subject to capital gains tax on immovable property in South Africa, and on equity shares when 80 percent or more of the market value of those shares is attributable directly or indirectly to immovable property in South Africa. The SARS tax guide for share owners states both that 80 percent test and a second test: the person, alone or together with a connected person, directly or indirectly holds at least 20 percent of the equity shares. The public "Tax and Non-Residents" page states the 80 percent test and does not repeat the 20 percent holding test. Both pages are SARS pages. The conflict is flagged below{src("sars_nr","sars_shares","sars_cgt")}.</li>
<li><strong>The treaty.</strong> Article 13 says gains from the alienation of real property situated in the other country may be taxed in that country. For this treaty, real property situated in the other country includes an equivalent interest in real property in South Africa. Article 6 says income from immovable property situated in the other country may be taxed in that country{src("irs_treaty")}.</li>
<li><strong>Withholding when a non-resident sells.</strong> Section 35A, as the SARS external guide describes it, makes the buyer withhold tax from the price paid to a non-resident seller of immovable property in South Africa. The guide sets the withholding at 7.5 percent if the seller is a natural person (SARS writes 7,5 percent), 10 percent if the seller is a company, and 15 percent if the seller is a trust. It applies when the amount payable is more than R2 million, and then it applies to the full price. It does not apply when the amount payable is R2 million or less. The amount withheld is an advance payment toward the seller's normal tax for that year, not the final tax. The seller can apply for a directive so that less, or nothing, is withheld. The buyer who is a South African resident pays SARS within the period the guide states for a resident, and a non-resident buyer has a longer period. Read the guide for those day counts and for the penalty if the buyer does not pay{src("sars_35a")}.</li>
<li><strong>Rent.</strong> SARS says the source of rental income is generally where the property is used day to day. Rental from South African property, received by a non-resident, is subject to normal tax in South Africa. Expenses such as rates and taxes, bond interest, insurance and repairs may be claimed, subject to conditions SARS states. Article 6 allows South Africa to tax that income as well. A United States resident who is also taxed on it in the United States looks to the foreign tax credit{src("sars_nr","irs_treaty","irs_ftc")}.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>I am still a director</h2>
<div class="card"><p><strong>Can a non-resident be a director?</strong> A sentence that a director must live in South Africa was not found on the Companies and Intellectual Property Commission (CIPC) or gov.za pages checked for this guide. What those pages do say is that a foreign national can be appointed, and that CIPC runs a foreigner-assurance step before the appointment. From 1 December 2023, CIPC's notice says a foreign national submits information and a certified passport or identity document on e-Services before being appointed as a director of a company, close corporation or co-operative. The appointment steps for a passport holder are in CIPC's director-amendment guide. A 2023 amendment of the Companies Regulations, published on gov.za, requires a director who is not South African to be recorded with a passport number, the country of issue, and the country of birth where that differs from the country of issue{src("cipc_foreign","cipc_steps","gov_regs")}.</p>
<p><strong>South African tax on directors' fees.</strong> The SARS guide for employers in respect of employees' tax for the 2026 tax year says the definition of employee includes a director of a private company, so remuneration paid to that director is subject to employees' tax. The same guide says that where the non-executive director is a non-resident, the directors' fee is regarded as remuneration and the employer must deduct employees' tax. The certificate code guide describes code 3621 as directors' remuneration received from a South African source by a non-resident non-executive director{src("sars_paye","sars_codes")}.</p>
<p><strong>The treaty.</strong> Article 16 says directors' fees and other remuneration that a resident of one country derives for services rendered in the other country, in the capacity of a member of the board of a company that is a resident of that other country, may be taxed in that other country. The article is about services rendered in the other country. Whether fees for work done from the United States were "rendered in" South Africa is not decided on these pages{src("irs_treaty")}.</p>
<p><strong>United States tax, and work permission.</strong> Permission to work is an immigration question. USCIS says refugees may work from the day they arrive. Tax is a different question. If you are a United States resident alien, Publication 519 taxes you on worldwide income, which includes directors' fees from a South African company. The two rules sit side by side. One does not cancel the other{src("gc","irs_p519")}.</p></div>
<h2>Should I move my cash or leave it in South Africa?</h2>
<div class="card"><p>There is no official page that answers that question for you. These are the rules that bear on it.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>If you are still a South African tax resident,</strong> the single discretionary allowance and the R10 million foreign capital allowance are the SARB routes summarised on {L("moving-money/index.html","Moving money and pensions",f)}. The rand amount of the single discretionary allowance is in conflict between the April 2026 circular and the older public guides. Do not pick a number from memory{src("sarb_circ","sarb_guide")}.</li>
<li><strong>If you have ceased to be a South African tax resident,</strong> SARB's individuals guide sets the transfer rules: tax compliance status, the R10 million calendar-year figure, a stricter path above that, a once-off travel allowance in the year you cease, and a once-off cash balance of not more than R100 000 without reference to SARS. SARS asks, for an international-transfer approval, for proof that you have ceased and for the capital gains calculation on the deemed disposal{src("sarb_guide","sars_ait")}.</li>
<li><strong>The account itself.</strong> SARB defines a non-resident rand account. It does not, on the pages checked, order you to close a South African account when you cease to be a tax resident{src("sarb_guide")}.</li>
<li><strong>United States tax on the interest.</strong> A resident alien is taxed on worldwide income. Interest from a South African account is part of that income. You report amounts on the United States return in United States dollars. The IRS says you translate foreign-currency items at the exchange rate when you receive, pay or accrue them{src("irs_p519","irs_fx")}.</li>
<li><strong>Reports.</strong> The same account can trigger FinCEN Form 114 if the aggregate of foreign accounts exceeded $10,000 at any time in the year, and Form 8938 if the specified-asset thresholds are met. See {L("foreign-accounts/index.html","Reporting SA accounts",f)}{src("irs_fbar","irs_8938")}.</li>
<li><strong>A gift or an inheritance from outside the United States.</strong> The IRS says a United States person files Form 3520 to report, among other things, gifts or bequests of more than $100,000 from a nonresident alien individual or a foreign estate, and gifts from a foreign corporation or foreign partnership that exceed the threshold the IRS publishes for section 6039F. That second threshold is on the IRS inflation-adjustment page the filing-requirements article points to. This guide does not copy a figure it did not open on that list. The same IRS article says a foreign trust can include a foreign pension or retirement plan that is treated as a trust. Form 8938 instructions say Revenue Procedure 2020-17 can exempt some tax-favoured foreign retirement trusts from Form 3520, and that the exemption does not remove Form 8938{src("irs_3520","irs_i8938")}.</li>
<li><strong>A gain that exists only because the rand moved.</strong> The IRS foreign-currency page tells you how to translate income and expenses into dollars. It does not, on that page, set out a separate rule for a gain or loss that arises only because cash sat in a rand account while the exchange rate moved. That rule is left out. Ask a practitioner if it matters to the account{src("irs_fx")}.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>Conflicts and gaps</h2>
<div class="card conf"><ul>
<li>GILTI and NCTI: the December 2025 Form 5471 instructions and the About Form 8992 page, reviewed on 22 July 2026, say GILTI. The IRS draft forms page posted draft Instructions for Form 8992 on 21 October 2026. The file prints 19 October 2026, is marked draft and not for filing, and its heading uses the revision label December 2026. That draft says the name is net CFC tested income for taxable years beginning after 31 December 2025. December 2026 is the label on the draft, not a month that has arrived{src("irs_5471","irs_8992","irs_8992dft","irs_drafts")}.</li>
<li>Land-rich shares: the non-residents page states an 80 percent market-value test. The share-owners guide states that test and a 20 percent holding test. This guide does not drop either sentence{src("sars_nr","sars_shares")}.</li>
<li>The single discretionary allowance rand amount is in conflict. See {L("moving-money/index.html","Moving money and pensions",f)}{src("sarb_circ","sarb_guide","sarb_faq")}.</li>
<li>A Companies Act rule that a director must live in South Africa: not found on the pages checked. The pages that were found describe how a passport holder is appointed{src("cipc_foreign","gov_regs")}.</li>
<li>Whether directors' fees earned while you sit in the United States are from a South African source: the certificate code is described as South African-source remuneration, and Article 16 is about services rendered in the other country. The pages do not apply those sentences to remote work{src("sars_codes","irs_treaty")}.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>What these pages leave out</h2>
<div class="card"><ul>
<li>Any recommendation to move cash, to keep it, to sell property, or to resign a directorship.</li>
<li>The inflation-adjusted FBAR penalty in dollars. The IRS says the statutory maximums are adjusted annually and does not need this guide to invent the current figure{src("irs_fbar")}.</li>
<li>A statement that an exit tax on an ordinary employee is usually small. SARS does not say that{src("sars_cease")}.</li>
<li>State income tax, other than the Florida personal-income-tax sentence on the {L("tax-first-year/index.html","checklist",f)}.</li>
</ul></div>''')
